---
description: |
  Stamps the OCSF record_integrity profile on fully derived + cast
  records: attestation_list[0] with a SHA-256 fingerprint over the
  canonical serialization of the stored shape, chained via prev_event.
  Runs AFTER ocsf_derive + ocsf_cast so the fingerprint covers the
  record exactly as stored — the verification lane (verify.tql) then
  recomputes it from stored read-backs.
  The chain scope is the caller's: `chain_uid` names the chain this
  record joins, evaluated per record (OCSF objects/attestation.md —
  chain_uid is "Stable for the lifetime of the chain. For example, every
  attestation produced during a single agent session shares one
  chain_uid"). The investigations wrapper pins it per case
  (metadata.correlation_uid, the case spine); the detection plane pins
  it per node (the node identity constant the chart renders), one chain
  per node in time order per
  docs/issues/tenzir-record-integrity-attestations.md.
  The chain linkage mode is the caller's too, and the split is
  load-bearing (live-probed on v6.19.1 against a 100k-record batch):
  `chain="caller"` — the caller supplies EVERY record's tail in
  `prev_tail` (uid, type_uid, fingerprint of the chain's previous
  record). Works when the caller paces records one at a time (the
  investigations HTTP ingest: the context write lands inside the
  inter-request gap). Against a fast batch the context reads run
  engine-batches ahead of the writes and every link goes stale — so the
  batch lanes use `chain="stream"`: the operator chains the stream
  itself in (time, uid) order via its internal lag, the OCSF-specified
  two-attestation shape — attestation_list[0] is the record fingerprint
  (no prev_event: computable per record with no cross-record state, so
  the lag can carry it forward), attestation_list[1] is the chain link
  (prev_event referencing the previous record's record fingerprint;
  carried un-hashed, fingerprint being a recommended-not-required
  attribute — the engine offers no channel to hash it at the link's
  position without reintroducing the ordering wall, so its integrity is
  enforced by the verify lane's adjacency check instead; link deletion
  masquerades as a chain restart — the documented limit). The caller's
  `prev_tail` in stream mode is the batch SEAM: the first record of the
  pass links to the previous pass's last record through it (the
  per-record context read is stale intra-batch, but the first read of a
  pass always sees the pre-pass state — probed), records 2+ chain via
  the lag and ignore it.
  A record with no tail at all is chain genesis when the caller's
  `genesis` expression says so; any other no-tail record is the
  documented node-restart case (chain state is per-node-lifetime
  memory) and warns below.
args:
  named:
    - name: authority_uid
      type: string
      description: The attesting node identity (OCSF authority_uid; the
        chart-rendered node name — tenzir-node-investigations,
        tenzir-node-core).
    - name: chain_uid
      type: string
      description: The chain this record joins, evaluated per record —
        the case spine for the investigations plane, a per-node identity
        constant for the detection plane.
    - name: genesis
      type: bool
      description: Per-record expression — true when a missing chain
        tail legitimately STARTS a new chain (the investigations case
        open, class_uid? == 2005 and activity_id? == 1); false when
        every no-tail record is a chain (re)start (the detection plane,
        whose first record follows every node boot or restart).
    - name: chain
      type: string
      default: caller
      description: caller — the caller supplies every record's tail in
        prev_tail (paced lanes, the investigations ingest). stream — the
        operator chains the batch in (time, uid) order itself; prev_tail
        is only the batch seam, the first record of the pass linking to
        the previous pass's last record (fast batch lanes, the archive
        rule).
---

// The attesting identity must name a party: a null authority_uid would
// stamp integrity theater (an attestation that names nobody).
assert $authority_uid != null, message="attest requires authority_uid: the attesting node identity"

// Idempotence guard: a record that already carries an attestation (e.g.
// a read-back replayed through the ingest lane) passes through unstamped.
if attestation_list? == null {
  if $chain == "stream" {
    // ---- stream mode: the record fingerprint first --------------------------
    // attestation_list[0] carries NO prev_event, so its fingerprint is a
    // pure function of the record — no cross-record state, nothing for the
    // batch-async context to get stale about. That is what makes the chain
    // buildable inside one fast pass at all: the lag below can carry this
    // fingerprint to the next record because it exists BEFORE the lag's
    // position (the OCSF hash rule — objects/attestation.md, "the entire
    // event, including the attestation's own authority_uid, chain_uid, and
    // prev_event attributes, and excluding only the attestation's
    // fingerprint and signatures" — is what forbids the simpler
    // single-attestation shape here: prev_event rides INSIDE the hash, so
    // a chained single attestation is strictly sequential and the engine
    // has no cross-position state channel for that; probed live, the
    // context version of this mis-links every record of a fast batch).
    //
    // The claim that the record carries this profile rides BEFORE the hash
    // input is captured, so the claim itself is covered by the fingerprint.
    metadata.profiles = append(metadata?.profiles?, "record_integrity")
    // The caller's prev_tail (the batch seam — the previous pass's last
    // record) rides the record into the operator, so the hash input NULLS
    // it out explicitly: the verify recompute nulls the same field the
    // same way (the stored record carries no prev_tail — attest drops it
    // below), and print_json of a null field is byte-stable on both
    // sides. A live probe of the naive shape (hashing the seam in)
    // mismatched every fingerprint on read-back — the null-out is
    // load-bearing, not cosmetic.
    attestation_list = [{
      authority_uid: $authority_uid,
      chain_uid: $chain_uid,
    }]
    _hash_input = print_json(({
      ...this,
      prev_tail: null,
    }).sort())
    attestation_list = attestation_list.map(a => ({
      ...a,
      // The fingerprint object (vendored objects/fingerprint.md): the
      // algorithm (3 = SHA-256), the value encoding (1 = Hex), and the
      // serialization scheme (99 = Other, source-defined — NOT JCS:
      // print_json pretty-prints with two-space indents, not the RFC 8785
      // minimal form; "In the case of Other, it is defined by the event
      // source").
      fingerprint: {
        algorithm_id: 3,
        encoding_id: 1,
        serialization_id: 99,
        serialization: "tenzir-sorted-json",
        value: hash_sha256(_hash_input),
      },
    }))
    drop _hash_input

    // ---- stream mode: the lag, then the chain-link attestation --------------
    // lag follows input order within its pipeline (reference/operators/
    // lag.md), and the caller sorts the batch on (time, metadata.uid) —
    // the same order the verify walk re-sorts on — so the injected tail is
    // the immediately preceding record of this batch. The value expression
    // reads the record fingerprint, which the stage above already
    // attached: born before the lag's position, the one ordering the
    // engine actually guarantees in-stream.
    lag value={
      uid: metadata.uid,
      type_uid: type_uid,
      fingerprint: attestation_list[0].fingerprint,
    }, into=_prev

    // The effective tail: the lag tail (records 2+), the seam (record 1
    // of the pass), or neither — chain genesis, or the documented
    // restart case, warned below: the fork keeps the record flowing on
    // the main branch; the assert drops only the branch copy with a
    // WARNING diagnostic, and the discard sinks the branch (a fork
    // branch must have one).
    fork {
      where _prev? == null and prev_tail? == null and not $genesis
      assert false, message="record_integrity chain restart: no chain tail for this record (node lost in-memory chain state); chain continues from here with prev_event absent"
      discard
    }
    if _prev? != null or prev_tail? != null {
      // The chain-link attestation (attestation_list[1]): prev_event
      // binds this record to its predecessor — prev_event rides absent
      // on genesis per objects/prev_event.md ("Absent on the first, or
      // genesis, event of a chain"). It carries no fingerprint of its
      // own (recommended, not required — objects/attestation.md): the
      // engine's only cross-position state channel is the lag above,
      // which delivers the predecessor's fingerprint AFTER this
      // record's record-fingerprint was hashed; hashing the link would
      // need the link's own fingerprint in the NEXT record's input, the
      // same ordering wall one level down. The verify lane enforces the
      // link by adjacency instead; a deleted link attestation
      // masquerades as a chain restart — the documented limit.
      attestation_list = append(attestation_list, {
        authority_uid: $authority_uid,
        chain_uid: $chain_uid,
        prev_event: _prev? if _prev? != null else prev_tail,
      })
    }
    drop _prev?, prev_tail?
  } else {
    // ---- caller mode (the investigations shape) -------------------------------
    // The caller supplies the chain tail for this record in prev_tail
    // (uid, type_uid, fingerprint of the chain's previous record); a
    // null/absent tail on a non-genesis record is the documented
    // node-restart case. The fork keeps the record flowing on the main
    // branch: the assert drops only the branch copy with a WARNING
    // diagnostic in the node's diagnostics stream, and the discard
    // sinks the branch (a fork branch must have one).
    fork {
      where prev_tail? == null and not $genesis
      assert false, message="record_integrity chain restart: no chain tail for this record (node lost in-memory chain state); chain continues from here with prev_event absent"
      discard
    }

    // The attestation, pre-fingerprint: OCSF rule (vendored
    // tenzir-ocsf/v1.9.0, objects/attestation.md) — "The attestation is
    // computed over a canonical serialization of the event: the entire
    // event, including the attestation's own authority_uid, chain_uid,
    // and prev_event attributes, and excluding only the attestation's
    // fingerprint and signatures." So: assemble the attestation WITHOUT
    // the fingerprint, hash that serialization, attach the fingerprint
    // last. prev_event rides absent on genesis per objects/prev_event.md
    // ("Absent on the first, or genesis, event of a chain") — a null
    // field would serialize differently from a missing one and break
    // read-back recomputation. chain_uid is the caller's chain scope.
    if prev_tail? != null {
      attestation_list = [{
        authority_uid: $authority_uid,
        chain_uid: $chain_uid,
        prev_event: prev_tail,
      }]
    } else {
      attestation_list = [{
        authority_uid: $authority_uid,
        chain_uid: $chain_uid,
      }]
    }
    // prev_tail is pipeline plumbing, not stored content: the verifier
    // recomputes over the stored shape, so it must leave before the hash
    // input is captured (prev_event above already carries the copy).
    drop prev_tail?

    // The claim that the record carries this profile rides BEFORE the hash
    // input is captured, so the claim itself is covered by the fingerprint.
    metadata.profiles = append(metadata?.profiles?, "record_integrity")
    _hash_input = print_json(this.sort())
    attestation_list = attestation_list.map(a => ({
      ...a,
      fingerprint: {
        algorithm_id: 3,
        encoding_id: 1,
        serialization_id: 99,
        serialization: "tenzir-sorted-json",
        value: hash_sha256(_hash_input),
      },
    }))
    drop _hash_input
  }
}
